6,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,9885,279,6783,400,74,3,773,429,279,38999,315,279,1346,370,19186,400,88,284,856,61,17,488,595,3,323,400,87,284,379,61,17,488,595,3,525,68660,311,1817,1008,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the constant $k$ so that the graphs of the parabolas $y = x^2 + k$ and $x = y^2 + k$ are tangent to each other.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6d23767f0cfd4168c8a86e18d3e7d959fd973151b304e46700fd59fe92b2e9be"},"seconds":64.55982899665833,"seed":532981992372446777,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the constant \\( k \\) such that the graphs of the parabolas \\( y = x^2 + k \\) and \\( x = y^2 + k \\) are tangent to each other, we need to ensure that they touch at exactly one point and have the same slope at that point.\n\nLet's denote the point of tangency as \\( (a, b) \\). Since the point lies on both parabolas, we have:\n\\[ b = a^2 + k \\]\n\\[ a = b^2 + k \\]\n\nFirst, we substitute \\( b = a^2 + k \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ a = (a^2 + k)^2 + k \\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying:\n\\[ a = a^4 + 2a^2k + k^2 + k \\]\n\\[ a = a^4 + 2a^2k + k^2 + k \\]\n\nRearranging terms to form a polynomial equation:\n\\[ a^4 + 2a^2k + k^2 + k - a = 0 \\]\n\nFor the parabolas to be tangent, the point of tangency must satisfy this equation, and the slopes at that point must be equal. The slope of the tangent line to \\( y = x^2 + k \\) at \\( (a, b) \\) is given by the derivative:\n\\[ \\frac{dy}{dx} = 2a \\]\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to \\( x = y^2 + k \\) at \\( (a, b) \\) is given by the derivative of the inverse function:\n\\[ \\frac{dx}{dy} = 2b \\]\nThus, the reciprocal of this slope is:\n\\[ \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1}{2b} \\]\n\nSetting the slopes equal:\n\\[ 2a = \\frac{1}{2b} \\]\n\\[ 4ab = 1 \\]\n\\[ b = \\frac{1}{4a} \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( b = \\frac{1}{4a} \\) back into the equation \\( b = a^2 + k \\):\n\\[ \\frac{1}{4a} = a^2 + k \\]\n\nNow, substituting \\( b = \\frac{1}{4a} \\) into \\( a = b^2 + k \\):\n\\[ a = \\left( \\frac{1}{4a} \\right)^2 + k \\]\n\\[ a = \\frac{1}{16a^2} + k \\]\n\nWe now have two equations:\n1. \\( \\frac{1}{4a} = a^2 + k \\)\n2. \\( a = \\frac{1}{16a^2} + k \\)\n\nFrom the first equation:\n\\[ k = \\frac{1}{4a} - a^2 \\]\n\nSubstitute \\( k \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ a = \\frac{1}{16a^2} + \\left( \\frac{1}{4a} - a^2 \\right) \\]\n\\[ a = \\frac{1}{16a^2} + \\frac{1}{4a} - a^2 \\]\n\nMultiply through by \\( 16a^2 \\) to clear the denominators:\n\\[ 16a^3 = 1 + 4a - 16a^4 \\]\n\\[ 16a^4 + 16a^3 - 4a - 1 = 0 \\]\n\nTo find \\( a \\), we test possible rational roots. Testing \\( a = \\frac{1}{2} \\):\n\\[ 16 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^4 + 16 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^3 - 4 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) - 1 = 16 \\left( \\frac{1}{16} \\right) + 16 \\left( \\frac{1}{8} \\right) - 2 - 1 \\]\n\\[ = 1 + 2 - 2 - 1 = 0 \\]\n\nThus, \\( a = \\frac{1}{2} \\) is a root. Substituting \\( a = \\frac{1}{2} \\) back into the equation for \\( k \\):\n\\[ k = \\frac{1}{4 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)} - \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^2 \\]\n\\[ k = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\\[ k = \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( k \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}} \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
